reasoning:
All three pass their own tests and get the main spec right: output formats, exit codes, ids that are never reused, atomic writes via a temp file and rename, and leaving a corrupt file untouched.

X ranks first. It has the cleanest structure (pure command functions with a single load and save in main), the most complete tests (exact assertions, checked setup steps, corrupt-file cases across contents and commands, out-of-order sort, id reuse after clear-done) and the best README. Its weaknesses are robustness only: raw stack traces on I/O errors and no checks on individual todos in hand-edited files.

Y matches X on spec correctness and is the most robust. It fully validates the data file and reports unexpected errors cleanly. Its tests are much thinner, though: no temp-dir cleanup, unchecked setup steps, and missing cases such as the out-of-order sort. Its README is also minimal, so it ranks second.

Z ranks last. Its tests and README are good and it avoids pointless writes, but:
- it reports '-1' as 'invalid id' (exit 2), not 'not found' (exit 1), which goes against the spec's integer rule;
- on malformed todo items it crashes or writes NaN/null ids to disk;
- it does not clean up the temp file when a write fails;
- it shows raw stack traces on I/O errors.

That combination of a spec deviation and weaker data integrity puts it below Y, even though its tests are better.

All three pass their own tests and implement the core spec, including atomic writes and leaving corrupt files untouched. The ranking comes from edge cases and robustness.

Y and Z are close.
- Y wins on correctness for 'highest id ever used + 1': it rebuilds nextId from the highest stored id, so it survives hand edits, while Z and X trust a stored counter.
- Y also avoids needless writes and has broader corrupt-data tests (7 wrong-shape and invalid-JSON contents across every command, plus a README replay).
- Y has no hygiene defects. Z has a stale comment pointing to non-existent .team/notes files.
- Z has the best IO-error handling (friendly messages without paths or stacks), the safe-integer guard, and the most user-friendly README. That keeps it a close second.

X is the cleanest and smallest code, but it has real gaps:
- negative ids are reported as 'invalid id' rather than 'not found';
- shallow shape validation lets {"todos":[null]} crash with a stack trace and {"todos":[1]} print '#undefined';
- IO failures (TODO_FILE pointing at a directory, or a missing parent folder) dump raw Node stack traces;
- the test suite is noticeably thinner;
- the README is minimal.

Final ranking: Y > Z > X.

**Z ranks first.** It passed every spec check and edge probe I ran and handled the hard cases correctly:
- early 413 on a streamed body;
- control-character rejection and percent-encoded Location for non-ASCII URLs;
- hashed constant-time token comparison;
- a bounded rate limiter;
- traversal and malformed paths.

Its structure is clean and modular, it has the most thorough tests (83, including process-level and raw-socket cases), and the README is excellent. Its only weaknesses are minor: some duplicated tests, one weak assertion, and an external font request.

**Y versus X was close.**
- **X** is more correct on one real edge case: it redirects non-ASCII and CR/LF URLs properly, where Y returns 500 after counting the click.
- **Y** is better engineered almost everywhere else:
  - it sends 413 early instead of draining an unbounded upload as X does;
  - its rate limiter does not grow without bound under a flood;
  - HEAD does not inflate clicks (in X it does);
  - its tests are stronger (fake-DOM render tests that forbid innerHTML, safeHref, chunked 413, a real-process entry point);
  - its README is much fuller;
  - its UI is more complete.

Y's bug is narrow and fails closed: a clean 500 with no header injection. X's weaknesses are spread across robustness, tests and docs. Holistically, Y ranks above X.
